<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Joseph Needham</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1900">1900</a>–<a href="/years/1995">1995</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Noel Joseph Terence Montgomery Needham</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Joseph Needham was a British biochemist, historian of science and sinologist who began the multivolume Science and Civilisation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. He studied at Gonville and Caius College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, and first worked on embryology, publishing Chemical Embryology in 1931. Taught Chinese by Lu Gwei-djen, he directed the Sino-British Science Co-operation Office in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chongqing">Chongqing</a> from 1942 to 1946. In 1948 he proposed to Cambridge University Press a book on science in China that grew into a long series of volumes, fifteen of them written by himself. He drew attention to what became known as the Needham Question, of why China ceded its leadership in science and technology to Western countries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/joseph-needham.jpg" alt="Joseph Needham" width="330" height="460" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kognos · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Joseph_Needham_in_Cambridge_1965_04.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Joseph Needham was born on 9 December 1900, the son of a Harley Street doctor and a music composer from County Meath. As a boy he heard the Sunday lectures of Ernest Barnes, to whom he later attributed his Christian faith and his openness to other religions. At Oundle School, under the headmaster Frederick William Sanderson, he gained a grounding in engineering and learned that knowledge of history was needed to build a better future. He graduated from Gonville and Caius College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, in 1921 and took his PhD in 1925, having turned from medicine to biochemistry under Frederick Hopkins.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Needham became a fellow of his college and worked in Hopkins's laboratory on embryology and morphogenesis. His three-volume Chemical Embryology of 1931 included a history of embryology, later published separately in 1934. He was elected a fellow of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-society">Royal Society</a> in 1941. With Julian Huxley he was one of the founders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>, where he argued that most of its support should go to countries outside the European and American centres of science. In 1936 he helped found a History of Science Committee in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, and he married the biochemist Dorothy Moyle in 1924.</p><p>In 1948 he proposed to Cambridge University Press a book on science and civilisation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Within weeks it had grown to seven volumes, and his first collaborator was the historian Wang Ling. The first volume appeared in 1954. Needham wrote fifteen volumes himself, and the series, of which 27 volumes have been published, continued after his death. He was Master of Gonville and Caius College from 1966.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>In The Grand Titration (1969) he posed what became known as the Needham Question: why modern science took its rise only in the West, when Chinese civilisation had long been more efficient in applying natural knowledge to practical needs. His work lists Chinese inventions such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cast-iron">cast iron</a>, the stirrup, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> and the magnetic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnetic-compass">compass</a>. Some scholars, among them Pierre-Yves Manguin, think he tended to exaggerate Chinese achievements and assume Chinese origins. Needham was a high church Anglo-Catholic who also called himself an honorary Taoist. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a> on 24 March 1995, aged 94.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Joseph Needham” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The historian of science <b>Joseph Needham</b> argued that Daoism’s attention to nature made it the seedbed of Chinese science.</blockquote><a href="/religion/daoism">Daoism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Joseph Needham</b>’s studies of Chinese science and technology asked why a comparable revolution did not take place in China, a question much debated and by some historians rejected as wrongly posed.</blockquote><a href="/movement/scientific-revolution">Scientific Revolution · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>In modern scholarship <b>Joseph Needham</b> used the term Legalism in 1954 for a positive-law reading of fa, but since Creel’s Legalists or Administrators? of 1961 many scholars have avoided it, and some regard it as inaccurate.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/legalism">Legalism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>Niels Bohr is credited with finding inspiration in yin and yang symbolism, while <b>Joseph Needham</b> argued that the book had impeded scientific development.</blockquote><a href="/work/i-ching">I Ching · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Joseph Needham</b>, writing in 1954, noted that twenty-three of the thirty Shang rulers' names appear on the Anyang bones, and concluded that Sima Qian had fairly reliable materials.</blockquote><a href="/work/records-of-the-grand-historian">Records of the Grand Historian · Reception</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1961" aria-label="London on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4547 381 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Joseph Needham"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4997 651h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4997 651h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1961">What was here in 1961? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
